First, I launch an Ubuntu EC2 instance and install AWS CLI, kubectl, eksctl, and Helm. I attach an IAM Role to the EC2 instance so it can securely 
communicate with AWS services without storing access keys. Then I create the EKS cluster using eksctl, verify the worker nodes with kubectl get nodes, 
and configure cluster access using EKS Access Entries. After that, I create a dedicated monitoring namespace, add the Prometheus Community Helm repository, 
and install the kube-prometheus-stack Helm chart. This deploys Prometheus, Grafana, Alertmanager, Node Exporter, and kube-state-metrics. Finally,
I verify the pods and services, access Grafana using port forwarding or a LoadBalancer service, retrieve the admin password from the Kubernetes secret, 
and use Grafana dashboards to monitor cluster CPU, memory, disk, pods, and nodes.


===================================================================================================================================================================


Interview Answer

"In one of my hands-on projects, I configured Prometheus and Grafana on an Amazon EKS cluster to monitor the Kubernetes infrastructure."

Step 1: Create a Jump Host (EC2 Instance)
First, I launched an Ubuntu EC2 instance that acted as a jump host (bastion host). I used this server to manage the EKS cluster and execute Kubernetes commands securely.

Step 2: Install Required Tools
On the EC2 instance, I installed the required tools:
AWS CLI
kubectl
eksctl
Helm

These tools are required for:

AWS CLI → Connect to AWS services.
kubectl → Manage Kubernetes resources.
eksctl → Create and manage EKS clusters.
Helm → Install Kubernetes applications like Prometheus and Grafana.

Step 3: Attach IAM Role
Instead of storing AWS Access Keys on the EC2 instance, I created an IAM Role and attached it to the EC2 instance.
For my practice environment, I attached AdministratorAccess.
But in production, we never give Administrator access. We follow the Principle of Least Privilege and assign only the required IAM permissions.
This is an important interview point.

Step 4: Create the EKS Cluster
Then I created the EKS cluster using eksctl.

eksctl create cluster \
--name demo-eks \
--region ap-south-1 \
--nodegroup-name linux-nodes \
--node-type t3.medium \
--nodes 2

This command creates:

EKS Control Plane
Worker Nodes
VPC
Security Groups
IAM Roles
Auto Scaling Group

After the cluster was created, I verified the worker nodes.

kubectl get nodes

I ensured that all nodes were in the Ready state.

Step 5: Configure Cluster Access
Next, I configured cluster access using EKS Access Entries.
This allows IAM users or IAM roles to authenticate and access the Kubernetes cluster securely.

Step 6: Create a Monitoring Namespace
To keep monitoring resources isolated from application workloads, I created a dedicated namespace.
kubectl create namespace monitor
This is a Kubernetes best practice.

Step 7: Add Helm Repository
Then I added the Prometheus Community Helm repository.
helm repo add prometheus-community https://prometheus-community.github.io/helm-charts
helm repo update

Step 8: Install Prometheus and Grafana
Using Helm, I installed the kube-prometheus-stack.
helm install kube-prometheus-stack prometheus-community/kube-prometheus-stack \-n monitor

This single Helm chart deploys multiple monitoring components automatically:

Prometheus
Grafana
Alertmanager
Node Exporter
kube-state-metrics

Step 9: Verify Pods
After installation, I checked whether all monitoring components were running.
kubectl get pods -n monitor
All pods should be in the Running state.

Step 10: Retrieve Grafana Password
Grafana stores the admin password inside a Kubernetes Secret.
I retrieved it using:
kubectl --namespace monitor get secrets kube-prometheus-stack-grafana \-o jsonpath="{.data.admin-password}" | base64 -d ; echo

Step 11: Expose Grafana
Initially, the Grafana service type was ClusterIP, which is accessible only inside the cluster.
I changed the service type to LoadBalancer.
kubectl edit svc kube-prometheus-stack-grafana -n monitor

Changed:

type: ClusterIP

to

type: LoadBalancer

Kubernetes automatically provisioned an AWS Elastic Load Balancer.
I also allowed TCP port 3000 in the Security Group.
Then I copied the Load Balancer DNS and accessed Grafana from my browser.

Step 12: Login to Grafana

Username: admin
Password: Retrieved from the Kubernetes Secret.

Step 13: Verify Monitoring
Finally, I verified that Prometheus was collecting metrics and Grafana dashboards were displaying:

Node CPU utilization
Memory utilization
Disk usage
Pod health
Node status
Kubernetes cluster metrics
Container metrics

This confirmed that the monitoring stack was working successfully.
