If the interviewer asks, "How does Prometheus collect metrics?"

You can answer:
Prometheus continuously scrapes metrics from Kubernetes components at regular intervals. The Node Exporter collects operating system metrics such as CPU, 
memory, and disk usage. kube-state-metrics exposes Kubernetes object information like deployments, pods, replicas, and namespaces. Prometheus stores all these
metrics in its time-series database, and Grafana connects to Prometheus as a data source to visualize them using dashboards.

If the interviewer asks, "Why did you use Helm?"

Answer:
Helm is the package manager for Kubernetes. Instead of manually creating multiple Deployment, Service, ConfigMap, Secret, and RBAC YAML files, Helm installs 
and manages all the required Kubernetes resources with a single command. It also makes upgrades and rollbacks much easier.

If the interviewer asks, "Why did you create a separate namespace?"

Answer:
I created a separate monitor namespace to isolate monitoring resources from application workloads. This improves organization, simplifies access control, 
and makes it easier to manage or upgrade the monitoring stack independently.

If the interviewer asks, "Why change ClusterIP to LoadBalancer?"

Answer:
By default, Grafana is exposed as a ClusterIP service, which is only accessible within the Kubernetes cluster. To access Grafana from a web browser outside 
the cluster, I changed the service type to LoadBalancer. In AWS, this automatically creates an Elastic Load Balancer and provides a public DNS endpoint.
